      "problem": "What is the slope of the line $4x-2y=1?$",
      "worked_solution": "We can find the slope of the line if we can express its equation in slope-intercept form. To do this, we solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2y & =1 \\\\ -2y & =-4x+1 \\\\ 2y & =4x-1 \\\\ \\frac{2y}{2} & =\\frac{4x-1}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{4x}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =2x-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the slope-intercept equation, we can tell that the slope of the line is $m=2.$",
